# t1049 — `for i, c in s.enumerate()` bounds the loop on the String's BYTE
# length while reading the element with a CODEPOINT indexer.
#
# "héllo" is 5 codepoints in 6 bytes. `lower_for_enumerate` reads the
# iterator's `.len` field (`Projection::Field(2)`) — for a String that is
# the BYTE length — so the loop runs 6 times, and the 6th element read
# indexes codepoint 5, which does not exist:
#
#   trap[T_Bounds]: str index out of bounds   rc=101
#
# after having printed all five correct pairs.
#
# ⚠ ACCIDENTALLY CORRECT ON ASCII (SIX Q#6). Byte length == codepoint count
# for every ASCII string, so every ASCII `.enumerate()` fixture in the
# corpus passes and none of them says anything about this.
#
# REFERENCE LAGS THE SELF-HOST: `self_host_lowerer/lower_loops.gg`'s
# `lower_for_string(… is_enumerate …)` handles enumerate on the STRING path
# with its own codepoint-ordinal counter and gets this right. Fix Rust as
# oracle hygiene.
#
# SIBLING SHAPE, same root, not separately filed: binding the element
# (`for (i, c) in s.enumerate(): held = c`) ICEs the compiler at
# `src/ir/lowering/mod.rs` with a Tier 2a "untracked source consumed"
# consume-site violation, because the String receiver takes the
# `infer_collection_element_type` fall-through instead of a String arm.
#
# INTENDED: the five pairs, exit 0.

void main():
    String s = "héllo"
    for i, c in s.enumerate():
        print(i)
        print(c)
